WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.248
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.577 --> 00:00:08.410
LEONIDAS KONTOTHANASSIS:
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.870
My name is Leonidas
Kontothanassis.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.100
I'm an engineering
director, at Google,

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.480
in charge of Cloud networking--

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:17.720
multiple products
fall in my team.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.470
The one that I'm going
to talk about today

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.500
is our Cloud CDN
offering, and I hope

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.880
you're all well caffeinated.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.860
This is probably a
more technical talk

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.320
than might have seemed
otherwise-- so lots

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.350
of technical details.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:33.280
So get ready.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.290
OK, so how is this talk
going to be organized?

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.920
I'm going to talk a little
bit about our Cloud network.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.390
This is not about the CDN.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.515
This is the network itself.

00:00:43.515 --> 00:00:45.598
The reason I'm going to
spend a little bit of time

00:00:45.598 --> 00:00:48.180
covering is it has affected
many of the decisions

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.070
we've made on how we build it--

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:53.520
how we built the CDN
offering on top of it,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.860
and now I want to make
another part clear.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.502
And then I'm going to move onto
the content delivery aspects.

00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:00.960
I'm going to spend some time
talking about the architecture

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:01.900
of our CDN--

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.379
you know, the platforms
that we used to build it,

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.660
and the logistics management--

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.930
how we make sure that
machines stay up,

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.880
performing well,
getting upgraded,

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.920
replaced, et cetera, et cetera.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.160
I'm going to spend some
time on our mapping system

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.220
that is the heart of the CDN.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.620
This is the system
that decides how

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.050
users get routed to
the right machines,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.520
and how traffic
from those machines

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.650
get routed back to users.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.080
Those two decisions
are independent.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:35.460
I'm going to spend a little bit
of time on the product itself,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.437
like the features that
we support and some

00:01:37.437 --> 00:01:39.270
of the features that
we're hoping to support

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:42.690
in the not-too-distant future--

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.880
talk about performance
and how we measure

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.250
performance internally
and also externally,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.950
and finally close with
a small demo showing

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.610
the benefits of having
CDN delivery for a, say,

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.545
graphics-heavy site.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.890
So this is a depiction
of the Google Network.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:04.360
Google Network changes, of
course you know, all the time.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.830
Currently, we have 20
regions with compute cycles

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.220
available in them, so those are
places where you can go and get

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.060
storage computation like the
whole heavy cloud machinery.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.130
We have 125 PoPs.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.910
Those are locations where
we run our Edge systems,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:23.950
and where we do caching.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.449
These are places where you can
meet our network for private

00:02:27.449 --> 00:02:29.740
interconnect, for instance,
if you're trying to connect

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.970
your on-prem facility to us,
but we will use them heavily

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.160
for our CDN offering--

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.700
and 13 sub cables connecting
all those facilities

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.360
to one another.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.510
It's one of the unique
features, of Google,

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.870
in that we run a global private
network unlike other cloud

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.720
providers, which typically
run isolated instances of data

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.760
centers in many locations.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.880
We run one global network
that connects them all.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.140
This has multiple advantages.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:57.640
It gives you
private connectivity

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.620
between machines that
are running, say,

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:02.360
in Asia to those that are
running in Europe or the United

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.330
States.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.990
And we use it heavily, as
well, to do various tricks

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.550
around traffic
routing, GRE, MPLS,

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:16.940
to be able to store traffic
that may have entered

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.630
our network in one location
to another location within it.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:24.610
That that is preferable
for serving purposes.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:26.410
This is another
view of our network.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.200
This is actually showing the
serving locations that we have.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.040
There is 90 of those--

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.410
90 different cities
around the world, which

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:37.340
sit on the edge of the network.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.330
Those are the ones that we
use both for Google and Cloud

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.180
CDN serving.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.030
They are very large deployments,
typically multiple petabytes

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.350
of cache ability, but we
have a much larger footprint

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:53.830
that sits off net inside ISPs.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.960
So there's about 4,000
of those deployments.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:59.560
They tend to be a lot
smaller in terms of size.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.980
Today, Google traffic is
roughly half and half.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:04.660
About half the
traffic that Google

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.170
has for its internal properties
comes from the on-net nodes--

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:10.900
about half comes from
the off-net nodes.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.630
Performance differences
between those two deployments

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.630
is actually highly
variable by country.

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:17.980
For instance, in the US, we
find that be on-net nodes

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.079
do far better than the off-net.

00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:24.820
Some places like Africa or Latin
America, the inverse is true.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:26.750
Some places like
Europe, the difference

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.710
then tends to be
practically negligible.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.720
We don't see much
of a difference.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.060
The reason that
we have both is we

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.790
want to have as good a
relationship with ISPs

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.140
as we can.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:42.130
In many of those
cases, ISPs see benefit

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:43.650
in having caches
in their network.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.100
In other cases, they don't.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.980
We play along with
whatever suits them best,

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.650
so we picked the best
interconnect capability

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.382
that fits our ISP partners.

00:04:53.382 --> 00:04:55.090
We are working to make
the off-net caches

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.200
available to Google Cloud.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.730
I'll say a little bit
more about that later,

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:00.320
but right now only
the on-net ones

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.750
are available for
the Cloud offering.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.810
OK, our Cloud CDN--

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:10.290
so we account for--

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:13.020
our Google and Cloud
CDN, we account for a lot

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.970
of the internet traffic.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:19.170
According to Sandvine, Google
is responsible for about 25%

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.040
of all internet bits.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.200
85% of that comes from
YouTube, but the other parts

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:26.350
are actually significant.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:30.156
And Cloud is the fastest
growing part of that traffic.

00:05:30.156 --> 00:05:31.530
It's growing faster
than YouTube.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.170
It is growing faster
than the other sources

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.970
of traffic for Google.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:39.420
We are seeing more than
50% year-over-year growth--

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.500
demand from over 130 countries.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.320
We have had to deal with
an enormous catalog size,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.360
so this informed
the decisions on how

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.610
we built those Edge caches.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:54.934
The primary reason for that is
that the most common content

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:56.100
that we're serving is video.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.930
Video tends to be very large.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.885
And user-generated video, which
is what we get out of YouTube,

00:06:00.885 --> 00:06:03.570
really is probably
the largest catalog.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.410
It's multiple hexabytes of data.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.700
So as I said before,
Cloud traffic

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:10.836
is the one that is
rising the most rapidly,

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:12.210
and that's the
one where we think

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:16.200
that we have to invest
the most heavily

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:20.184
to help our Cloud customers
get the benefits of our CDN.

00:06:20.184 --> 00:06:21.600
And the infrastructure,
as I said,

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:27.850
is shared between Google
and Cloud traffic.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:32.200
So we've built our CDN with
the following principles.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.880
Number one is ease of use,
so the key innovation here

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.027
is that we have a single IP
for serving content worldwide.

00:06:42.027 --> 00:06:44.110
There's a lot of technology
that goes behind that.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:45.990
I will spend some
time describing

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:49.000
how that technology works.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:52.990
Why would a customer care?

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:57.580
There is, oftentimes
especially for media partners,

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:01.330
we found that they want to
work with ISPs to either

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:05.260
flat rate, or low rate,
or have a deal that

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.480
allows their content
to be charged

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.500
as part of an ISP contract
like a cellular contract.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:14.802
Typically, ISPs know how to do
that only through IP addresses.

00:07:14.802 --> 00:07:16.760
They want to say, OK,
tell me what IP addresses

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.790
your client is
going to come from,

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:22.450
and being able to give them one
makes you their best friend.

00:07:22.450 --> 00:07:24.980
So it's very easy for
them to configure one IP.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.830
This is the content
where it's coming from.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:28.640
I know exactly how
to deal with that.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:31.990
The more you give them, the more
difficult their life becomes.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.180
We made it very easy to
activate our Cloud CDN.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.010
If you are already
on Google Cloud--

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:36.859
yes, sir?

00:07:36.859 --> 00:07:39.154
AUDIENCE: Is this a P4?

00:07:39.154 --> 00:07:40.727
IPv6 or IPv4?

00:07:40.727 --> 00:07:42.810
LEONIDAS KONTOTHANASSIS:
The question is, can this

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.250
be an IPv6 or IPv4?

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:45.750
The answer is both.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:48.630
You can choose.

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:52.260
So we have a
single-click activation

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.360
on top of our global
load balancer.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.680
So if you've got your backend
sitting inside of Google Cloud,

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:01.460
and you decide that you
want cache delivery,

00:08:01.460 --> 00:08:04.244
it's a trivial turn on.

00:08:04.244 --> 00:08:05.910
Just one click, I
will actually show you

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.890
the configuration page.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:13.380
We care a lot about security,
so by default all delivery

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.870
is done over SSL with
no additional cost.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:19.650
We have also other programs
for configuring certs,

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:22.830
and uploading them,
and managing them.

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:26.940
Your backends, the ones that
live inside the Google Cloud,

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:29.070
are unexposed to the internet.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:33.169
So if you bring up your VMs,
or you have a backend storage

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:35.210
packet, by default
that one does not

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:38.340
have to be exposed to
the internet traffic.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.580
The CDN takes all
of the connection

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:44.159
and then connects through
Google's private network

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:48.180
to those backends, and it's got
a built-in SYN-flood defense.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:51.360
So because the backends are
not exposed to the internet,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.620
any attack will stop
at the proxy layer.

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.070
We have seen the largest
attacks seen in the history

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:01.850
of the internet--
we believe and--

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:03.390
you know, size helps.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:06.230
When your carry about 25%
of all internet traffic,

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.220
it's kind of hard
for you to come down.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:09.810
And finally, you
know performance

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.190
has been another guiding force.

00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:12.898
Obviously, we care
about that internally.

00:09:12.898 --> 00:09:16.470
We think that latency is
critical to the success of web

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.760
delivery.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.450
We invest heavily on
state-of-the-art protocols,

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.904
so our CDN supports HTTP/2.

00:09:22.904 --> 00:09:24.570
That was something
that we built and now

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.800
is part of industry standards.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:30.930
It supports QUIC, which is
not yet an industry standard

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:32.280
but is going through the IETF.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.980
And of course it's supported
by Chrome and Mozilla.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.490
And we have open libraries if
you want to build your own app,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.800
and we have
measurements that show

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:44.220
that we have industry-leading
performance in both latency,

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.390
availability, and throughput.

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:50.140
So more of that coming later.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:54.380
OK, so what does
our CDN look like?

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.960
So at a particular
metro, we have a layer

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.810
of packet forwarders,
where traffic

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.250
is coming into our network.

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:07.360
The name for those packet
forwarders is Maglev.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.030
There is a very good
SIGCOMM paper from 2016

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:12.250
that describes the behavior.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:15.324
Maglevs can do multiple
millions of packets per second.

00:10:15.324 --> 00:10:16.490
They don't have to do a lot.

00:10:16.490 --> 00:10:18.190
What they have to
do is figure out

00:10:18.190 --> 00:10:21.220
where the right destination
for a particular packet is

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:22.900
and send it there.

00:10:22.900 --> 00:10:25.170
Typically, it's right next
to them but not always.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.590
I'll get more to that later.

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:30.580
Then we have our L1 proxy.

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.500
That is, essentially,
the caching proxy

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.300
that sits at the
edge of the network.

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.770
There is three layers of cache
sitting right next to that L1

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:40.390
proxy.

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:43.420
There is an in-memory cache for
the hottest possible, you know,

00:10:43.420 --> 00:10:44.650
part of the content.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:49.090
That tends to be on the order of
single digits of terabytes only

00:10:49.090 --> 00:10:51.280
used for something
that is extremely

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:55.180
hot that needs to be served, you
know, at very, very high rate.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:57.940
There is what we
call medium cache.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:01.180
That is on the order of
triple digits of terabytes--

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:02.320
depends on the location.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:05.830
Demand isn't always the same for
all locations on the internet.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:07.690
It's based on Flash.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.900
We use it very heavily for small
contents that are very latency

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:14.230
sensitive but also some
big content that, you know,

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:17.616
ends up being hot enough to
merit promotion to this cache.

00:11:17.616 --> 00:11:19.990
And then there is finally what
we call large cache, which

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:21.850
is based on spinning disk.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:24.220
That is typically on
the double-digit orders

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:26.080
of petabytes.

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:28.180
It was built with
YouTube in mind.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:32.770
We need a cache that gives us
98%, 99% hit rate on YouTube,

00:11:32.770 --> 00:11:36.050
so it had to be fairly
large to accommodate that.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:39.430
This hierarchy of
caches is what's

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.292
being made available
to our Cloud customers.

00:11:42.292 --> 00:11:45.700
The L1 proxies will
obviously look things up

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:48.940
in their local cache hierarchy
to see if it's available.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.710
If not, then they
will actually go back

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:54.640
to the instances that
have the original content.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.420
Depending on the
kind of instance,

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:58.150
there may be another
layer of caching.

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:01.210
It is what we call
a shield cache.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:03.270
Not all origins require that.

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.020
For instance, if your
origin happens to be

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:06.010
Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:08.620
Google Cloud Storage is a
fairly big system in itself.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:11.560
It does not need protection,
so there the L1 proxies

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:12.980
will go to it directly.

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.300
But if you're running your
single VM, for instance,

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:19.381
there will be a layer of caches
that would aggregate requests

00:12:19.381 --> 00:12:21.880
coming from the first layer,
so that you don't get hammered.

00:12:24.626 --> 00:12:25.500
How do we build this?

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:30.580
These are actually pictures
of racks out in the field.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:33.480
So the way we
build it is we have

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.090
a very modular
horizontal architecture,

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:40.650
so what we do if we want to
augment a particular location,

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.860
we just add another rack,
which both increases storage--

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:46.350
since, you know, racks
are integrated storage

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:49.350
and egress bit pumps
and it increases

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:51.690
in our egress capability.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:55.800
We tend to upgrade our machines
when they hit utilization

00:12:55.800 --> 00:13:00.450
of about 70%, and we have a
fairly sophisticated logistics

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:03.540
program to make sure that
just-in-time augmentations

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:05.520
happen correctly.

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:09.380
And the limiting factor here
is the breadth of network

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:14.040
here, so those machines
plug routers or switches

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:15.850
at the edge of the network.

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:20.790
So the breadth of that gear is
one of the limiting factors,

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:24.300
and it is one of the things
that forced us to do work.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:30.390
So up until a few years ago,
we were using regular routers

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:35.070
as sold by people like Cisco,
Juniper, possibly others.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.400
But what we were observing was
that the port density on those

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:43.200
was increasing slower than
the traffic was increasing,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.410
and we were limited both
by cost and scalability

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:49.160
if we were to continue
to use that technology.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:50.910
So we kind of looked
at them and said, you

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:52.290
know, what does a router do?

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:54.060
Well, you know it does BGP.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:56.140
It exchanges information,
routing information

00:13:56.140 --> 00:13:57.390
with the rest of the internet.

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:58.410
It does packet forwarding.

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:00.326
When it gets a packet,
it knows how to send it

00:14:00.326 --> 00:14:02.400
to the right final destination.

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.974
It does ACLs, so
packets, that are not

00:14:04.974 --> 00:14:06.390
supposed to go out
to the internet

00:14:06.390 --> 00:14:09.015
or are not supposed to come into
our network from the internet,

00:14:09.015 --> 00:14:10.980
get dropped at that point.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:17.237
And it does this in a not very
effective packaging from a cost

00:14:17.237 --> 00:14:19.320
perspective, so we wanted
to take all those things

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:23.730
and put them together into
something that would be

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:25.990
both more scalable and cheaper.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:30.060
So the solution to that is the
system that we call Espresso.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.166
A full paper on
Espresso has been

00:14:32.166 --> 00:14:33.540
published in this
year's SIGCOMM,

00:14:33.540 --> 00:14:37.350
for anybody that was interested.

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:40.690
Espresso comes with three parts.

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.130
The first is a peering fabric.

00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:45.970
The peering fabric is
effectively a switch.

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:49.900
It has no routing smarts in it.

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:52.740
It just knows how to connect
sort of our side of the network

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:56.790
to the rest of the internet.

00:14:56.790 --> 00:14:58.770
It's much cheaper,
because it doesn't

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:01.530
have all these extra
capabilities that routers do,

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:04.560
and it is much more dense.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.890
You can get about 10x the
number of ports on a switch

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:09.130
than you can get on a router.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:11.040
So now that you
have this switch,

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:14.530
you need some way to
ship traffic through it,

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:17.920
so what we have is we have moved
all of the stuff in software.

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:19.890
So we ran on our
own BGP listener

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:22.620
on the servers that
are sitting on the Edge

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:24.720
to get the routes
from the other side,

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:28.620
and we pass that information
to our mapping system.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:31.030
And I will explain
how that works.

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:32.850
We run two layers
of controllers--

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:36.120
a global controller that
determines where traffic is

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:40.230
supposed to go, and that global
controller produces assignments

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:43.300
about once a minute, which
it ships to the servers,

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:44.460
and it tells them--

00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:46.560
for this particular
destination, I

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:49.860
need you to send it out to
that switch on that port.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:53.580
And we have mechanisms
implemented,

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:57.480
using GRE and PLS, that allow
us to do that with the switches

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:58.440
that we have.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:00.690
And we have a local
controller that essentially

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:02.460
reacts on the order of seconds.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:03.960
Routes on the
internet come and go.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:05.430
Somebody may withdraw a route.

00:16:05.430 --> 00:16:07.410
He might say, you
know, I can't be

00:16:07.410 --> 00:16:09.757
reached at this point from
this particular location.

00:16:09.757 --> 00:16:11.340
The local controller
is the one that's

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:13.680
supposed to take that
information and say, OK,

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:16.350
where else can I send it until
the global controller can

00:16:16.350 --> 00:16:19.110
compute a more
optimized assignment

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:21.450
for that destination?

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:25.530
And all of the packet processing
that the routers were doing

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:28.050
has moved to the CPUs.

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:30.420
Earlier, I showed you
a picture of something

00:16:30.420 --> 00:16:33.270
that we called Maglev that
was doing packet processing

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:35.310
on the incoming side.

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:38.620
Maglev has been
extended, and now it

00:16:38.620 --> 00:16:39.870
has a different internal name.

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:41.400
It can do packet
processing on both

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.830
the incoming and
the outgoing side,

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:48.400
so we can send the packets
to the right destination.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:53.430
So logistics, the difficulty
here comes from the fact

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:58.080
that we're running a very
large fleet in many places.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:02.910
We have about 5% of our machines
in some kind of repair need

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:06.599
at any point in time, and this
requires active management

00:17:06.599 --> 00:17:09.709
for a fleet that has tens
of thousands of servers.

00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:11.250
If you were to do
that manually, that

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:16.140
would be a very high burden
to our operations side,

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:18.900
so we have taken
extensive efforts

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:22.589
to automate all of this
work, including upgrades,

00:17:22.589 --> 00:17:25.730
installations, and repairs.

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:28.970
All of that work happens
through a portal, where

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:32.330
we meet with our ISP partners.

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:36.050
And we, through
that portal, have

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:40.550
workflows that allows us to do
dozens of turnups every week--

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:42.540
dozens of repairs every week.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:47.330
And in more than 90%
of those, no Googler

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.100
actually has to touch anything.

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.330
It's all automated.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:52.440
So a machine gets shipped.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:56.210
It gets connected either
by a contract worker

00:17:56.210 --> 00:17:58.130
or by the ISP themselves.

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:01.070
And then from that point
on, the system automatically

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:04.240
takes over, it logs
into the machine,

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:07.170
installs the right software,
brings up the BGP session,

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:10.530
assigns it, brings it
into the mapping system,

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:12.260
and traffic starts flowing.

00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:15.380
And it knows how to do that
actually in a smart way,

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:17.870
so that traffic gets
brought up gradually,

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.750
so that we don't overwhelm
the backbone network-- filling

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:25.080
a very large cache with
a very large workload.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:30.140
So peering, this is
one of the key benefits

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:32.300
of working with Google.

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:36.830
We have invested very
heavily with our ISP partners

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:40.822
to make sure that we have the
best possible reachability.

00:18:40.822 --> 00:18:42.530
A lot of the time,
people think of a CDN,

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:44.810
and they think it is an
issue of machine capacity.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.450
Yes, machine capacity
matters, but what we find

00:18:47.450 --> 00:18:50.270
is that machine capacity
is something that

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:53.120
typically is controlled by us.

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:54.620
We get to ship the machines.

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:57.320
We get to install them.

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.080
It's an easier
problem to solve then

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:03.310
something that requires an
interlocutor on the other side.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.210
Bringing up additional
peering with a partner

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:10.220
means that both you and them
have to be in agreement,

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:12.240
and you have to coordinate.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:17.480
So for peering, we actively
manage and forecast

00:19:17.480 --> 00:19:20.360
our peering demand,
so we actually

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:23.720
look at what we think we're
going to need with our partners

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:25.830
not just over the
next three months,

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:27.860
but over the next three years.

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:31.730
And we communicate all of
that information to them

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:33.980
through the portal,
and we tell them

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:35.690
that this is the
location to where we see

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:37.820
the traffic is coming to you.

00:19:37.820 --> 00:19:39.200
We think we need augment.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:41.660
We think we're going to meet
the augment this many months

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:42.770
from now.

00:19:42.770 --> 00:19:45.170
We aim to have a
one-hop distance

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:47.930
to 98% of all ISPs
on the internet,

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:50.870
and those tend to be
the most major ones.

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:53.270
And we aim to have
multiple paths

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:54.890
into each one of those ISPs.

00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:57.380
So we are not content
in having just one place

00:19:57.380 --> 00:19:58.880
where we can connect with them.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:01.460
We want to connect to them in
as many places as possible.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:03.410
For the big ones, that's
actually fairly easy.

00:20:03.410 --> 00:20:07.556
They understand the
value of doing that.

00:20:07.556 --> 00:20:10.250
They see it for
their own benefit,

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:13.100
because if you can
connect in multiple places

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:16.620
then you can give them
content close to their users,

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:19.250
so they don't have to use
their own backbone to move

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.410
that content around.

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:24.740
For the smaller
ones, sometimes we

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.195
have to find alternate paths
through transit providers

00:20:27.195 --> 00:20:28.820
since they might not
be able to meet us

00:20:28.820 --> 00:20:31.200
in more than one location.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:34.730
And we use our mapping
system to actively manage

00:20:34.730 --> 00:20:38.986
those connections and steer the
traffic to the right connection

00:20:38.986 --> 00:20:39.860
at any point in time.

00:20:45.087 --> 00:20:45.920
The mapping system--

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:53.780
OK, so what is the
mapping problem here?

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.260
So the mapping
problem is that we

00:20:56.260 --> 00:21:00.310
have multiple paths to every
user just because of the way

00:21:00.310 --> 00:21:02.050
we're building the system.

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:06.070
We have billions of users
that we need to accommodate.

00:21:06.070 --> 00:21:07.570
Thankfully, those
billions of users

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:10.440
can usually be
subdivided to groups,

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:13.400
so you don't have to deal with
each one of them individually,

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:14.720
although we try to.

00:21:14.720 --> 00:21:18.260
I'll say more about that later,
but still millions of user

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:19.190
groups.

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:21.610
And demand, capacity,
and congestion changes

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:23.080
in real time, so
you need a system

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.206
that can deal with
all those constraints.

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:34.990
So what do we have to do?

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:38.280
We, essentially, have to
generate an assignment of users

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:39.990
to sites and links.

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:42.270
Remember here, it's not
enough to just send something

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:44.082
to a particular location.

00:21:44.082 --> 00:21:45.540
You also have to
know exactly which

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:48.330
links since we are peering
with multiple ISPs in that one

00:21:48.330 --> 00:21:49.470
location--

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:52.680
are going to get used
for this particular user.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.510
You have to respect
the available capacity

00:21:54.510 --> 00:21:58.410
of the machines and the
network, so the supply

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:00.310
is often greater than--

00:22:00.310 --> 00:22:02.160
it has to be greater
than the demand, which

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:03.692
is not always true.

00:22:03.692 --> 00:22:05.400
You have to respect
the peer preferences.

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:07.732
Our peers, our ISP
partners, give us policy

00:22:07.732 --> 00:22:08.940
that they want us to enforce.

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:12.229
They say that I want, you know,
this particular set of users

00:22:12.229 --> 00:22:14.770
to be served from here, or this
other particular set of users

00:22:14.770 --> 00:22:17.340
to be served from
some other place.

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:20.280
We want to minimize cost, and
we want to maximize quality,

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:23.320
pursuant to those constraints.

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:27.750
So what does the mapping
system look like?

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:28.890
There's a number of inputs.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:31.590
Obviously, we get BGP and
all the other routing data.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:34.890
This essentially tells
us where the users are--

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:39.180
how far away they are from any
one point in network distance.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:40.980
Network distance
is number of hops,

00:22:40.980 --> 00:22:44.640
not necessarily what the
latency is between, sort of,

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:46.770
that location where
the user is, or what

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.560
the throughput is between
that location and the user.

00:22:49.560 --> 00:22:52.650
We augment that with our
own observations of latency.

00:22:52.650 --> 00:22:54.750
As we serve users, we
collect information.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:57.810
We constantly run a
small trickle of users

00:22:57.810 --> 00:22:59.880
to other locations--
what we call

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:01.380
spraying-- so that
we can see how

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:06.450
the ideal location, according to
BGP, compares with other ones.

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:09.030
The network topology, we
have built our own model

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:12.000
for both sort of our network and
what the internet looks like.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:14.280
We actually have a fairly
sophisticated model

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:15.480
for all major ISPs.

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:17.550
We share it with
them, and we tell them

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:19.870
where we see bottlenecks,
not only sort of--

00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:21.090
I mean, not on our side.

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:22.830
That we keep
internally and we fix.

00:23:22.830 --> 00:23:26.100
But we also tell them when we
see bottlenecks on their side,

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:28.630
and we work with them to fix it.

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:32.520
One of the things that was
published a few years back

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:34.110
was the user score.

00:23:34.110 --> 00:23:35.970
This is like a
YouTube number that

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:40.615
tells users and ISPs what
their score is in terms of sort

00:23:40.615 --> 00:23:42.870
of for video consumption.

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:46.260
We actually spent a
good couple of quarters

00:23:46.260 --> 00:23:49.200
working with the ISPs, before we
made it available to end users,

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:51.300
to make sure that they
had every opportunity

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:52.932
to fix their
networks and improve

00:23:52.932 --> 00:23:54.390
the quality and
the score that they

00:23:54.390 --> 00:23:58.234
would get on this before we
made it available to the world.

00:23:58.234 --> 00:23:59.400
And obviously load reports--

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.108
you know we want to
know how much traffic

00:24:01.108 --> 00:24:02.520
is going in any one place.

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:08.440
So then the system starts
with a link and machine limit

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:08.940
computation.

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:11.470
It determines what are the
capacities and the demand

00:24:11.470 --> 00:24:14.640
in, sort of, every
one of those elements.

00:24:14.640 --> 00:24:19.560
It does a preliminary candidate
selection, so it says,

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:21.600
OK, for this particular
user, it looks

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.964
like not the whole
world as a good choice

00:24:24.964 --> 00:24:26.130
for where to assign them to.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:29.790
But here is the top three,
or four, or five choices.

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:32.310
And then that is
fed to an optimizer.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:39.450
The first, sort of, parts of
the system are eager algorithms,

00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:40.260
which--

00:24:40.260 --> 00:24:43.320
greedy algorithms,
which simply do best

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:45.000
guesses for what those are.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:48.390
The optimizer is a far more
complicated piece of code.

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:50.310
It has linear programming
to try and find

00:24:50.310 --> 00:24:52.200
sort of the optimal solutions.

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:55.620
And we're trying to minimize
the size of the input data,

00:24:55.620 --> 00:24:59.100
so that it can run within
a one-minute interval.

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:01.230
We hope to produce
those assignments

00:25:01.230 --> 00:25:04.560
about once a minute,
so that we can

00:25:04.560 --> 00:25:07.800
shift traffic around
in response to changing

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.780
demand from the internet.

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:14.430
For safety reasons, we
archive every single one

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:17.040
of the outputs of all
of those subsystems.

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:19.350
If, at any point in time,
something goes wrong,

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:22.650
we want to be able to restore
the system to a sane state,

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.900
and say, OK, we're not
quite sure what is going on,

00:25:25.900 --> 00:25:29.310
but here is an assignment that
worked fine 15 minutes ago.

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:31.810
Please use that
and move forward.

00:25:31.810 --> 00:25:34.170
And finally, we
actually push that

00:25:34.170 --> 00:25:38.430
to a system that does a
resolution of the user request

00:25:38.430 --> 00:25:41.100
and gives them the location
that they should be going--

00:25:41.100 --> 00:25:43.290
that they should be going to.

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:48.300
So this is one area
where we have deviated

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:50.640
from what many other CDNs do.

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:54.420
Instead of relying on
an indirection, which

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:57.570
is quite common of
users to resolvers,

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:59.840
and then sort of figuring
out, from the resolver, what

00:25:59.840 --> 00:26:03.450
the user should be
assigned, we rely

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:05.970
on either direct integration
with the player for the YouTube

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:11.250
or on our AnyCast solution to
know exactly who the client is

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:12.895
and send them to
the right location.

00:26:12.895 --> 00:26:14.270
So in the previous
picture, where

00:26:14.270 --> 00:26:16.500
I was showing the
Maglevs, what that

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:20.760
would look like is a request
that arrives from a user

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:23.980
knows exactly the
client IP for that user.

00:26:23.980 --> 00:26:25.630
We don't need to
know the resolver.

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:27.040
The resolver is irrelevant.

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:29.670
Everything goes, always,
to the same IP anyway.

00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:33.990
So when the request packet
comes into our network,

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:37.830
it comes with a client IP,
and the forwarders look at it

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:42.000
and determine is the local
machine the optimal machine

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:43.927
to serve this piece of content?

00:26:43.927 --> 00:26:45.510
If the answer is
yes, then it's simply

00:26:45.510 --> 00:26:47.530
a hand off sort of
on the local machine,

00:26:47.530 --> 00:26:50.310
but some fraction of the
time that is not the case.

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:51.910
The mapping system
has determined

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:54.210
that another cache
is better suited

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:55.650
for serving this content.

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:57.540
It will then take that
packet and forward it

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:02.420
to that other cache, and
responses then come out direct.

00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:04.970
So the response is
asymmetric to the request,

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:08.040
so the response will
go out from a port that

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:10.890
is closest to this user, as
far as the mapping system was

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:12.068
able to determine.

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:21.070
So I already spoke the first
few bullets on this slide.

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:22.900
There is some
additional enhancements

00:27:22.900 --> 00:27:25.540
that we put into the mapping
system to make it perform

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:26.500
as well as possible.

00:27:26.500 --> 00:27:30.040
One is the dynamic
estimation of path limits--

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:32.470
what we call end-to-end limits.

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:35.470
What that feature does
is it tries to determine,

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:39.850
for any group of users, what
is the capacity of the internet

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:43.775
between sort of a cache and
where the end user lies.

00:27:43.775 --> 00:27:46.150
The way it does that
actually is-- we

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:50.872
have observed that the internet
has a one level of performance

00:27:50.872 --> 00:27:52.330
when it is lightly
loaded, which is

00:27:52.330 --> 00:27:55.019
very easy to determine because
it happens at trough times.

00:27:55.019 --> 00:27:57.310
In the middle of the night,
there's not too many people

00:27:57.310 --> 00:27:59.060
trying to use those connections.

00:27:59.060 --> 00:28:00.310
So it's never zero.

00:28:00.310 --> 00:28:03.850
There's always
someone, and I think

00:28:03.850 --> 00:28:05.890
the ratio between trough
and peak is sort of two

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:06.910
to one or so.

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:10.210
So there's still a lot of
demand at all times of the day

00:28:10.210 --> 00:28:12.730
and night, but because
it's so much lower,

00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:15.610
you can sort of assume that
the network is unburdened.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:18.460
And the performance that you are
observing at that point in time

00:28:18.460 --> 00:28:22.710
is indicative of the system
that is operating at its best

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:23.920
capability.

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:26.650
So then, what we
do is we constantly

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:28.510
observe the throughput
and the latency

00:28:28.510 --> 00:28:31.990
that we're seeing at
peak times, and if we're

00:28:31.990 --> 00:28:33.700
starting to see
that it is deviating

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:37.120
from that optimal observation
that we have seen at trough

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:40.750
times, then we kick in,
sort of, the algorithm

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:44.110
that spreads that
load to other paths.

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:46.510
And we measure those
other paths as well,

00:28:46.510 --> 00:28:50.470
so that we get an equalized
level of performance

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:51.790
across all the paths.

00:28:51.790 --> 00:28:53.092
There's no point-- yes, sir?

00:28:53.092 --> 00:28:56.466
AUDIENCE: So night in one
place is day in another, right?

00:28:56.466 --> 00:28:59.840
Night in one place
of the world is day

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.012
in another place of the world.

00:29:02.012 --> 00:29:03.220
So how do you suppress noise?

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:05.320
LEONIDAS KONTOTHANASSIS: Yes, so
night in one part of the world

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:07.090
is day in another
part of the world,

00:29:07.090 --> 00:29:08.650
so how do you suppress noise?

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:11.240
The reality is that
assignments are localized,

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:14.500
so an Indian user will be
sent to an Indian location,

00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:17.590
and a US user will be
sent to a US location.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:19.614
So night and day, they
are very different.

00:29:19.614 --> 00:29:22.030
And the measurement is actually
relative to the users that

00:29:22.030 --> 00:29:23.988
are supposed to that
local location, as opposed

00:29:23.988 --> 00:29:27.190
to somebody who is coming from
the other side of the world.

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:29.680
And another big
thing that we do is

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:31.360
we try to tell the users apart.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.520
Not every user has
the same capability.

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.740
The announcements that
we get from the internet

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:39.520
are coming in these large
blocks that we call CIDR blocks,

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:43.240
so oftentimes an ISP will say,
hey, here is half a million

00:29:43.240 --> 00:29:45.220
or, you know, a couple
of million users.

00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:47.797
Please route them sort
of this way for me,

00:29:47.797 --> 00:29:50.380
and the reality is those are not
always the same set of users.

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:52.370
They don't have the
same capabilities,

00:29:52.370 --> 00:29:54.520
so we constantly
observe, for each user,

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:57.730
what the capability is.

00:29:57.730 --> 00:29:59.830
And we try to divide
them in smaller groups,

00:29:59.830 --> 00:30:02.590
so that we can assign them
independently rather than

00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:06.970
treating them as one big lump
based on how the ISP advertises

00:30:06.970 --> 00:30:09.850
them to us.

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:13.990
So what we've seen-- as we've
been working on the mapping

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:17.050
system and improving it--

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:20.290
we have reduced the
amount of backbone usage,

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:24.580
like the traffic that has to
go from a particular cache

00:30:24.580 --> 00:30:29.080
to another exit point from
about 10% originally down

00:30:29.080 --> 00:30:30.850
to zero now.

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:32.410
We managed to
reduce our miss rate

00:30:32.410 --> 00:30:37.000
by properly assigning
requests to the right rack--

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:39.530
from about 4% to 2%.

00:30:39.530 --> 00:30:42.430
And we had one other
feature in our system, which

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:44.110
is if the mapping
system kind of screws

00:30:44.110 --> 00:30:46.570
up, the caches are allowed
to defend themselves

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:48.730
by redirecting
some traffic away.

00:30:48.730 --> 00:30:51.070
So this is only used
for YouTube, which

00:30:51.070 --> 00:30:52.460
has a very, very large demand.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:53.834
We don't use the
redirect feature

00:30:53.834 --> 00:30:55.540
for Cloud CDN and
our cloud customers

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:57.820
obviously, because we
don't control their client,

00:30:57.820 --> 00:31:00.940
and a redirect may end up
being harmful for them.

00:31:00.940 --> 00:31:03.804
But because YouTube is such
a large part of the CDN,

00:31:03.804 --> 00:31:05.470
being able to redirect
that traffic away

00:31:05.470 --> 00:31:08.170
leaves room for everybody else.

00:31:08.170 --> 00:31:11.020
And modeling the machines
and the disk capacities,

00:31:11.020 --> 00:31:17.260
we're able to reduce the
redirect rate from 3% to 1%.

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:20.410
And this mapping system
actually matters.

00:31:20.410 --> 00:31:22.750
On the ingress side, we
find that if you just

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:27.550
rely on BGP, which is the
closest hop, about 14%, 15%,

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:30.070
of the traffic would land in a
place that would not give you

00:31:30.070 --> 00:31:32.080
optimal performance,
so being able to take

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:35.350
these incoming requests and
forward them to the right cache

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:39.500
actually makes a difference
not for most of the traffic,

00:31:39.500 --> 00:31:43.100
but a good enough chunk
of the traffic to matter.

00:31:43.100 --> 00:31:45.300
So here is actually some
small performance results

00:31:45.300 --> 00:31:50.590
of turning on the aggregation
and end-to-end limits.

00:31:50.590 --> 00:31:53.260
It's experiments showing
rebuffer rates and retransmit

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:54.150
rates.

00:31:54.150 --> 00:31:58.690
You can see market
increases and decreases

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:03.550
in retransmit rates and
reductions in rebuffer rate

00:32:03.550 --> 00:32:05.920
when we turned those things on.

00:32:05.920 --> 00:32:08.990
So now that I've actually
covered a little bit

00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:11.440
on the technical, I
want to go a bit more

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:12.910
on the product philosophy--

00:32:12.910 --> 00:32:16.360
what is it that we're trying
to achieve with our Cloud CDN?

00:32:16.360 --> 00:32:19.900
So first of all, I want to
say that we are partners--

00:32:19.900 --> 00:32:22.420
we're a cloud company first and
foremost, not a CDN company,

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:25.600
so yes, we build our
own CDN because we

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:27.760
want to offer our
customers a one-stop shop.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:29.500
If they want to buy
something from us

00:32:29.500 --> 00:32:32.440
and have it all work
seamlessly and easy,

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:33.989
then they should
be able to do that.

00:32:33.989 --> 00:32:35.530
At the same time,
we fully understand

00:32:35.530 --> 00:32:38.140
that many of our customers,
that are coming to our Cloud,

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:40.180
already have existing
relationships,

00:32:40.180 --> 00:32:43.210
or they want features that
we are not offering yet.

00:32:43.210 --> 00:32:45.160
So we have a large
number of partners,

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:46.834
and we expect that
we will continue,

00:32:46.834 --> 00:32:48.250
for the indefinite
future, to have

00:32:48.250 --> 00:32:50.230
a large number of CDN partners.

00:32:50.230 --> 00:32:53.090
And our customers are
free to choose sort of us,

00:32:53.090 --> 00:32:54.820
them, a combination.

00:32:54.820 --> 00:32:58.060
Many of them want
to be multi-vendor.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:33:04.130
That should continue to be the
case for the indefinite future.

00:33:04.130 --> 00:33:07.570
However, we want to make
ours as easy to use,

00:33:07.570 --> 00:33:10.060
and we hope that
customers will see

00:33:10.060 --> 00:33:18.190
the value of sticking with
a one-stop shop and pick us.

00:33:18.190 --> 00:33:22.750
Obviously, this decision
rests with the customers,

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:25.720
but we are making it as
easy to use as possible.

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:27.400
Here is, actually,
the configuration.

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:28.990
After you have a
backend, you can

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:32.560
see that the Enable CDN button
is just a single check box.

00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:35.490
So once you have configured
a backend inside Google,

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:37.800
you want to enable caching.

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:40.630
The configuration is about
as trivial as it gets.

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:42.610
Any other integration
will surely

00:33:42.610 --> 00:33:46.660
be more complicated than
that, I guarantee it.

00:33:46.660 --> 00:33:49.660
We offer easy
management-- log delivery

00:33:49.660 --> 00:33:52.810
inside the Cloud and
performance statistics

00:33:52.810 --> 00:33:56.360
all in the same Pantheon
view, where you will actually

00:33:56.360 --> 00:34:02.060
go and configure your CDN
and your other backends.

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:08.079
So again, if you are used
to having a partnership

00:34:08.079 --> 00:34:10.370
with Google, you are actually
using our other products,

00:34:10.370 --> 00:34:11.989
you're familiar with our tools--

00:34:11.989 --> 00:34:13.850
the same set of
familiar tools will

00:34:13.850 --> 00:34:17.960
be used to visualize and
control your CDN and the things

00:34:17.960 --> 00:34:21.110
that it generates.

00:34:21.110 --> 00:34:23.060
We've been adding
features to it,

00:34:23.060 --> 00:34:25.550
so that we are sort of
late starters in this game.

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:28.429
We initially focused very
heavily on infrastructure,

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:31.400
because we were building
something for internal use.

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:35.080
And now that we are
externalizing the CDN,

00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:37.219
we are finding that our
customers want more things

00:34:37.219 --> 00:34:40.429
than what Google needed
internally, so we've added

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:42.139
support for custom cache keys.

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:44.210
So if you decide
that you don't want

00:34:44.210 --> 00:34:47.840
your whole URL to be a key, then
you can select pieces of it.

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:49.457
We've added support
for signed URLs.

00:34:49.457 --> 00:34:51.290
This is something that
many of our customers

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:54.560
have asked for more
secure delivery.

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:59.270
We are partnering with
another Cloud product,

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:02.846
that we have called Anvato to
support live streaming if this

00:35:02.846 --> 00:35:04.220
is something that
you want to do,

00:35:04.220 --> 00:35:06.390
or you can just do live
streaming in your own way.

00:35:06.390 --> 00:35:08.030
But if you want a
package solution,

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:11.030
then we have a full
integration with Anvato.

00:35:11.030 --> 00:35:14.090
Obviously, we seek support that
wasn't specific to the CDN,

00:35:14.090 --> 00:35:18.300
but once we did it for all
Cloud, CDN got it for free.

00:35:18.300 --> 00:35:20.930
User-defined header insertion,
many of our customers

00:35:20.930 --> 00:35:22.982
have asked for
that, so we support

00:35:22.982 --> 00:35:24.440
certain sets of
headers that can be

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:26.990
inserted into their requests.

00:35:26.990 --> 00:35:30.495
You can support multiple
certs and managed certs

00:35:30.495 --> 00:35:32.670
as part of our CDN offering.

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:35.550
TLS cipher selection-- very
important for compliance.

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:40.250
Some customers only want to
use particular SSL ciphers.

00:35:40.250 --> 00:35:42.850
We give them the option of
selecting which ones they want.

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:45.890
Content takedown, that's
obviously a feature

00:35:45.890 --> 00:35:47.294
that every CDN needs to have.

00:35:47.294 --> 00:35:48.710
Customers sometimes
make mistakes.

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:51.010
They put up content that
they don't want to put up.

00:35:51.010 --> 00:35:52.468
If it's out in the
caches, you need

00:35:52.468 --> 00:35:54.050
to be able to get rid of it.

00:35:54.050 --> 00:35:56.750
And finally, one of the
more recent things--

00:35:56.750 --> 00:36:00.330
which is going GA
next week, it has

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:02.000
been announced for awhile now.

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:03.380
It's in open beta--

00:36:03.380 --> 00:36:05.650
is what we call large
object media support.

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:11.089
Our origins are in video,
and it was one of the things

00:36:11.089 --> 00:36:13.130
that-- our Cloud CDN
customers were complaining--

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:15.380
was even though we had great
infrastructure for video,

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:17.900
our initial implementation
for Cloud CDN

00:36:17.900 --> 00:36:21.140
had a limitation on object
size that has been taken away.

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:25.700
There's been a few things
that had to happen.

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:28.730
Because video content
tends to be fairly large,

00:36:28.730 --> 00:36:32.000
we don't want to fetch it all in
one fell swoop into the cache,

00:36:32.000 --> 00:36:35.480
so we chunk it into
smaller pieces.

00:36:35.480 --> 00:36:37.550
And we fetch the piece
that is necessary,

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:41.640
based on what is being
requested from the user side.

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:44.090
There is one contract
that we need to have

00:36:44.090 --> 00:36:47.690
between us and the customer.

00:36:47.690 --> 00:36:49.580
The customer has
to guarantee to us

00:36:49.580 --> 00:36:53.540
that they have an origin
that supports range requests

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:56.480
and will actually tell us
what the size of the object

00:36:56.480 --> 00:37:01.292
is when we first
do a range request.

00:37:01.292 --> 00:37:02.750
As we've been
deploying it, I think

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:06.500
we found about 0.01%
of our customers

00:37:06.500 --> 00:37:08.380
doesn't actually have
compliant origins.

00:37:08.380 --> 00:37:11.270
This is surprising that
they don't, but we've

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:13.460
disabled the feature for
those customers who are

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:15.090
working with them to fix it.

00:37:15.090 --> 00:37:17.180
And this is the thing that
has delayed us slightly

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:18.410
in declaring GA.

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:20.150
We want to make sure
that nobody gets

00:37:20.150 --> 00:37:23.120
harmed in the process
of rolling this out.

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:31.720
So a Next talk wouldn't be
interesting without announcing

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:34.250
some things that should
come down the pike.

00:37:34.250 --> 00:37:37.410
So the number one
thing that we've seen

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:41.295
is our customers are saying that
it's great that you have a CDN.

00:37:41.295 --> 00:37:43.420
It's great that it works
so well with Google Cloud,

00:37:43.420 --> 00:37:44.590
but you know what?

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:46.990
We have assets that live
outside of Google Cloud.

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:50.290
And most of them are talking
about their on-prem assets,

00:37:50.290 --> 00:37:52.610
and they're saying we
need support for that.

00:37:52.610 --> 00:37:55.360
And some of them are saying, OK,
and we also want multi-cloud.

00:37:55.360 --> 00:37:57.250
We want to be able to
run some stuff on you

00:37:57.250 --> 00:37:59.680
and some stuff on
your competitors,

00:37:59.680 --> 00:38:02.500
and maybe we would like to use
your CDN regardless of where

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:04.520
our origins are sitting.

00:38:04.520 --> 00:38:09.670
So we are working in
bringing that to Cloud CDN.

00:38:09.670 --> 00:38:11.620
I'm hoping that by some time--

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:14.501
the end of this year-- we'll
have something to announce.

00:38:14.501 --> 00:38:16.750
The other big thing that I
announced, sort of earlier,

00:38:16.750 --> 00:38:18.480
is we're working in
extending our Cloud

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:21.740
CDN to the off-net
fleet that we have.

00:38:21.740 --> 00:38:23.800
The difficulty
there is primarily

00:38:23.800 --> 00:38:25.085
how do you do AnyCast off net?

00:38:25.085 --> 00:38:26.710
You know, you don't
control the network

00:38:26.710 --> 00:38:29.996
like you do when you're
inside the Google environment.

00:38:29.996 --> 00:38:31.120
I think we have a solution.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:32.650
We are working with
our ISP partners

00:38:32.650 --> 00:38:36.160
to allow us to do
this AnyCast solution.

00:38:36.160 --> 00:38:38.660
They are very incentivized
to work with us.

00:38:38.660 --> 00:38:43.150
They want to have the traffic
that Cloud CDN generates--

00:38:43.150 --> 00:38:44.404
come from the off-net caches.

00:38:44.404 --> 00:38:45.820
They would rather
not have it come

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:47.945
through peering for those
that have off-net caches,

00:38:47.945 --> 00:38:52.000
so we think we have the good
working relationship that

00:38:52.000 --> 00:38:54.190
will allow this to happen.

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:55.690
Integration with
Cloud Armor, this

00:38:55.690 --> 00:38:57.064
is a completely
separate product,

00:38:57.064 --> 00:38:59.260
but it is part of
the security suite.

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:03.490
People want to be able to set
both sort of blocking rules

00:39:03.490 --> 00:39:05.860
for particular
IPs, or countries--

00:39:05.860 --> 00:39:11.050
or protection for SQL
injection, or cross

00:39:11.050 --> 00:39:12.370
scripting, or other things.

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:14.620
So we have support for that.

00:39:14.620 --> 00:39:17.440
It is now supported
sort of at the LB layer.

00:39:17.440 --> 00:39:20.850
We are working on extending
it to the caching layer.

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:22.780
The product has
already been announced.

00:39:22.780 --> 00:39:25.390
It is in, I think,
alpha or beta.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:28.426
Forgive me for not
remembering exactly.

00:39:28.426 --> 00:39:29.800
One other optimization
that we're

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:33.250
trying to add for customers,
especially those that

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:37.990
want to do live events with
very, very low latency,

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:43.480
is the assume public content
for optimizing miss rate.

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:47.600
Right now, what the CDN does
is when it gets a request,

00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:49.690
it doesn't know
whether the response

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:51.010
is going to be cachable or not.

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:54.760
It cannot know until the
origin has responded,

00:39:54.760 --> 00:39:58.030
because the information is
available in the headers.

00:39:58.030 --> 00:40:00.732
So because it has
to be conservative,

00:40:00.732 --> 00:40:02.440
and it doesn't want
to introduce latency,

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:05.574
when it gets, say, three
requests for the same thing

00:40:05.574 --> 00:40:07.240
it has to forward
them all to the origin

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:10.090
at the same time in
order to make sure

00:40:10.090 --> 00:40:13.520
that they don't get delayed.

00:40:13.520 --> 00:40:16.330
Under this mode, a
customer would guarantee

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:18.820
that the content
is always cachable,

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:21.880
so then you can forward
just the first request

00:40:21.880 --> 00:40:23.890
and leave all of the
other ones sort of waiting

00:40:23.890 --> 00:40:25.570
for the first answer to come.

00:40:25.570 --> 00:40:28.420
And then you can get that
answer back to all the users

00:40:28.420 --> 00:40:30.840
that have been waiting.

00:40:30.840 --> 00:40:33.520
And finally, we're
working with Anvato

00:40:33.520 --> 00:40:37.720
to build an end-to-end
low-latency live video support

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:39.130
system.

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:43.300
We have internal
demonstrations, which

00:40:43.300 --> 00:40:46.330
shows that we can go from
encoder to final serving

00:40:46.330 --> 00:40:48.471
in low single-digit seconds.

00:40:48.471 --> 00:40:50.470
We're hoping to make that
available to our Cloud

00:40:50.470 --> 00:40:51.250
customers as well.

00:40:56.460 --> 00:41:02.640
Performance-- we have a very
extensive set of evaluations

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:05.930
for how well we're doing.

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:09.480
The, probably,
most important one

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:11.700
is the one that we share
with our ISP partners,

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:14.310
because that helps us not
only sort of improve our site,

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:17.500
but it helps them improve
their site as well.

00:41:17.500 --> 00:41:22.864
So this is an example of
an ISP partner, ASN 7018.

00:41:22.864 --> 00:41:24.780
And for those of you who
are in this business,

00:41:24.780 --> 00:41:26.790
you should know who
that partner is.

00:41:26.790 --> 00:41:30.570
And what we do is we capture a
small fraction of all packets.

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:33.780
As we know, we send them
down, and we analyze sort

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:36.000
of those packets, both
from the outgoing side

00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:39.520
and on the incoming side,
to detect bottlenecks.

00:41:39.520 --> 00:41:44.880
So we also insert some
packets, duplicate packets

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:48.394
with reduced TTLs, so we can
see sort of where they reach

00:41:48.394 --> 00:41:50.310
the network on the other
side, and how long it

00:41:50.310 --> 00:41:53.670
takes for the ICMP
expire to come back.

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:56.700
And all that goes into sort
of an analysis system that

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:59.850
determines where the bottlenecks
in the communication are.

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:01.320
So this is an example.

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:05.340
The system auto adjusts,
and essentially takes

00:42:05.340 --> 00:42:07.020
the worst possible
path, and assigns

00:42:07.020 --> 00:42:11.305
that to be the reddest
part of the spectrum.

00:42:11.305 --> 00:42:13.680
In this particular case, the
reddest part of the spectrum

00:42:13.680 --> 00:42:17.370
is still 8 megabits per second,
as you can see from the slider

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:18.760
above.

00:42:18.760 --> 00:42:20.632
So this is a very
well-provisioned ISP,

00:42:20.632 --> 00:42:21.840
not one that has bottlenecks.

00:42:21.840 --> 00:42:25.000
Every user is getting still at
least 8 megabits per second,

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:28.110
which is more than good
enough for even ultra HD,

00:42:28.110 --> 00:42:30.210
but we have that
for ISP partner.

00:42:30.210 --> 00:42:32.760
We have thousands of them,
and we share that information

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:33.690
with them constantly.

00:42:33.690 --> 00:42:37.080
And we help build better
connectivity for us

00:42:37.080 --> 00:42:39.390
and also for them.

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:40.197
Yes, sir?

00:42:40.197 --> 00:42:42.072
AUDIENCE: Is there a
publicly published paper

00:42:42.072 --> 00:42:45.284
or a dataset [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:45.284 --> 00:42:46.950
LEONIDAS KONTOTHANASSIS:
No, this is not

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:48.840
a publicly available dataset.

00:42:48.840 --> 00:42:50.510
It is available to ISP partners.

00:42:50.510 --> 00:42:52.614
If any of you are
an ISP or a telco,

00:42:52.614 --> 00:42:54.030
then you can sign
up to the portal

00:42:54.030 --> 00:42:56.980
and get your own information.

00:42:56.980 --> 00:42:58.770
Unfortunately, for
various reasons,

00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:02.720
we cannot tell one ISP
information about what other

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:03.390
ISPs are doing.

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:10.550
Internal evaluations are great.

00:43:10.550 --> 00:43:13.940
The reality is
that we trust them

00:43:13.940 --> 00:43:15.590
more than we trust
anything externally,

00:43:15.590 --> 00:43:21.320
but we fully understand that the
people that want to use our CDN

00:43:21.320 --> 00:43:23.720
don't necessarily have to take
our word on how well we're

00:43:23.720 --> 00:43:24.920
performing.

00:43:24.920 --> 00:43:28.790
So we've commissioned a study
from an external partner,

00:43:28.790 --> 00:43:30.020
Cedexis in this case.

00:43:30.020 --> 00:43:33.590
Cedexis has this fairly
interesting system

00:43:33.590 --> 00:43:37.700
where they have clients
around the world living

00:43:37.700 --> 00:43:40.460
inside people's
browsers or apps,

00:43:40.460 --> 00:43:43.010
and what they do is
they've convinced

00:43:43.010 --> 00:43:44.750
a whole bunch of
websites to allow

00:43:44.750 --> 00:43:46.390
them to install a
bit of JavaScript

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:47.780
sort of on their website.

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:49.630
And after the website
has finished loading,

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:51.380
that JavaScript, which
belongs to Cedexis,

00:43:51.380 --> 00:43:54.660
can kick off
measurements against any

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:58.400
of a number of destinations and
see how well those destinations

00:43:58.400 --> 00:43:59.850
are performing.

00:43:59.850 --> 00:44:03.530
So it's a very interesting
measurement system.

00:44:03.530 --> 00:44:06.200
It's got millions of vantage
points or tens of millions

00:44:06.200 --> 00:44:08.012
of vantage points
around the world.

00:44:08.012 --> 00:44:09.470
And it's very
difficult to optimize

00:44:09.470 --> 00:44:11.760
for because the requests
are coming from everywhere,

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:15.450
so it's essentially a
depiction of the internet.

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:17.870
Cedexis is actually
publishing those reports.

00:44:17.870 --> 00:44:22.790
There is a much fuller
list of all the CDNs.

00:44:22.790 --> 00:44:24.830
This is a Cloud
conference, so I'm actually

00:44:24.830 --> 00:44:29.020
presenting the results for our
biggest cloud competitor, AWS

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:30.200
and CloudFront.

00:44:30.200 --> 00:44:33.450
But all the results for all
CDNs, including our own,

00:44:33.450 --> 00:44:34.760
can be found in that URL.

00:44:34.760 --> 00:44:37.110
I encourage you to go
and click on the link

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:39.410
and look at them for yourself.

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:41.930
We have results for latency.

00:44:41.930 --> 00:44:44.079
We have results for throughput.

00:44:44.079 --> 00:44:45.995
The early part of the
graph, that was actually

00:44:45.995 --> 00:44:50.630
a problem like a small
downtime in the Cedexis side,

00:44:50.630 --> 00:44:52.580
so for a couple of days
the data was missing.

00:44:52.580 --> 00:44:55.160
But then the graph
comes back up.

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:59.330
And availability-- one
interesting thing to note

00:44:59.330 --> 00:45:01.220
here is you can
tell that this is

00:45:01.220 --> 00:45:02.660
a real internet-based
measurement.

00:45:02.660 --> 00:45:04.590
Availability is
actually pretty bad.

00:45:04.590 --> 00:45:07.070
It's about 99%.

00:45:07.070 --> 00:45:09.470
If you look at sort
of the guarantees

00:45:09.470 --> 00:45:12.980
that you get from almost every
CDN, it's like multiple nine,

00:45:12.980 --> 00:45:14.030
including ourselves.

00:45:14.030 --> 00:45:16.700
We promised four nines,
but we promised four nines

00:45:16.700 --> 00:45:19.280
on the availability
of the caches.

00:45:19.280 --> 00:45:21.170
The internet is a
very nasty place,

00:45:21.170 --> 00:45:24.790
and this includes people that
are sitting behind broken Wi-Fi

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:27.350
at their homes, or
they turn the microwave

00:45:27.350 --> 00:45:30.990
on when the measurement
happens to be going on.

00:45:30.990 --> 00:45:32.690
So the failures that
you're seeing here

00:45:32.690 --> 00:45:34.760
is sort of the real
effects of what happens

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:36.069
sort of out there in the world.

00:45:36.069 --> 00:45:38.360
You still need the four nines
in the cache to get that.

00:45:38.360 --> 00:45:41.090
If you didn't have those four
nines in the cache level,

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:43.280
you'd be getting
something worse than that,

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:47.150
and that's where you see some
separation between the CDNs.

00:45:47.150 --> 00:45:49.070
The biggest contributor
to what you're seeing

00:45:49.070 --> 00:45:51.350
is what happens outside on
the internet, but the CDNs

00:45:51.350 --> 00:45:56.710
themselves actually contribute
to a certain extent as well.

00:45:56.710 --> 00:46:01.680
I have a small demo on
the benefits of CDN.

00:46:01.680 --> 00:46:05.370
So we've configured a backend.

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:10.420
For the purposes of this demo,
we put this backend in India,

00:46:10.420 --> 00:46:13.440
so it's really far
away from here.

00:46:13.440 --> 00:46:16.830
And we have the origin.

00:46:16.830 --> 00:46:20.194
Just for this demo we made the
origin publicly accessible,

00:46:20.194 --> 00:46:21.360
so we can go to it directly.

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:25.509
It is not blanketed
from the internet.

00:46:25.509 --> 00:46:27.050
And here, I've run
some trace routes.

00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:29.640
I actually ran them
right before I came in.

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:33.030
You can see that the origin's
about 307 milliseconds away

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:39.120
from here, and the CDN IP
that is caching the content

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:41.860
is about 4 milliseconds
away from here,

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:42.840
which is pretty good.

00:46:45.480 --> 00:46:46.780
So here's side by side.

00:46:46.780 --> 00:46:53.550
On the left side, you
have the CDN site.

00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:58.020
It is loading, and you
can see how fast it is.

00:46:58.020 --> 00:46:59.670
On the right side,
you have something

00:46:59.670 --> 00:47:01.080
coming from the origin.

00:47:01.080 --> 00:47:03.180
You can see how
much slower it is.

00:47:03.180 --> 00:47:20.010
Chrome has this very beautiful
feature called Developer Tools,

00:47:20.010 --> 00:47:21.510
and if you click
on the Network tab

00:47:21.510 --> 00:47:28.320
you can actually see how long
it takes for this page to show.

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:31.630
So on the CDN side, it
takes about 300 milliseconds

00:47:31.630 --> 00:47:38.500
to load a page that has
about 15 fairly large images.

00:47:38.500 --> 00:47:44.950
This is a CDN configuration
that includes QUIC,

00:47:44.950 --> 00:47:50.550
so it's our best protocol for
loading persistent connections.

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:53.760
The right-hand side is
sort of an origin that

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:58.320
is going over HTTP/11, so it's
not really a fair comparison

00:47:58.320 --> 00:48:01.730
but is a one that I created
to showcase the difference.

00:48:01.730 --> 00:48:02.430
I encourage you.

00:48:02.430 --> 00:48:05.310
The slides will be available
when you go back to your homes.

00:48:05.310 --> 00:48:06.970
Click on the CDN site.

00:48:06.970 --> 00:48:08.502
Run some trace routes.

00:48:08.502 --> 00:48:09.460
It will be the same IP.

00:48:09.460 --> 00:48:12.043
You'll be able to see that it's
always a few milliseconds away

00:48:12.043 --> 00:48:12.990
from you.

00:48:12.990 --> 00:48:16.890
With that, I'd like
to summarize and say

00:48:16.890 --> 00:48:20.830
that we focused on ease of
use, security, and performance.

00:48:20.830 --> 00:48:22.740
I'm not going to
repeat the bullets.

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:25.580
Those are sort of the
key aspects of the CDN

00:48:25.580 --> 00:48:27.660
that we want to share
with our customers.

00:48:27.660 --> 00:48:30.900
And I have an obligation
to also tell you

00:48:30.900 --> 00:48:34.260
that there is a lot of beautiful
networking talks up next.

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:36.750
I encourage you to attend
as many of them as you can

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:38.550
and learn about
the great network

00:48:38.550 --> 00:48:41.040
that Google Cloud has to offer.

00:48:41.040 --> 00:48:45.030
And since everybody likes
swag, if you want a beautiful

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:47.790
poster-- you know, nice, glossy
to put on your wall of, you

00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:50.300
know, how great the
Google network is--

00:48:50.300 --> 00:48:52.710
g.co/poster, put
your information,

00:48:52.710 --> 00:48:54.270
and we'll be happy
to give you one.

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:55.650
Thank you.

00:48:55.650 --> 00:49:00.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

